{"id":"143fc8aa-61f3-4a02-b6e4-42849f1ab526","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009866,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required = case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"]\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    candidates = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if n[\\\"removable\\\"]]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid][\\\"depth\\\"], nid))\\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required:\\n            found = False\\n            for rid in retained:\\n                if rid == nid:\\n                    continue\\n                if node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym:\\n                    found = True\\n                    break\\n            if not found:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. The task is SE-PE-01, which appears to be about grammar-guided node reduction (like Perses). The payload contains nodes forming a tree (each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). Also required_symbols list. The objective: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving grammar validity and interestingness (presence of required symbols). The output should be retained_node_ids (all nodes that remain) and removed_node_ids (order of removal). The algorithm: \"deeper nodes before shallower\", \"never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children\". \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that takes the payload (as dict) and returns {removed_node_ids: list, retained_node_ids: list} per the schema.\n\nInterpretation: The tree is defined by parent references. The root has parent null. Depth given. For removal, we consider removable nodes. We process nodes in order: deeper depth first (highest depth first), and for same depth, by id (alphabetical? stable tie break). For each candidate node, check if it is removable. Then check if it is root (depth 0, or parent null) - never remove. Also check if it is a required_symbol: symbol is in required_symbols? The requirement \"Never remove … a required symbol\" means if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, cannot remove. Also \"node with retained children\" means if the node has any children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then cannot remove it? Actually \"Never remove … a node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are not removed, you cannot remove it, because that would orphan them? But in tree reduction, you might remove a node and promote children? The description: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but seems like simple pruning? Actually Perses reduces by deleting nodes. But the \"retained children\" condition suggests you cannot remove a node if any of its children are still in the tree (i.e., not removed yet). That makes sense: you must remove leaf nodes first (or nodes whose children have already been removed). So algorithm: iteratively process removable nodes in depth order, but only if they have no children (or all children have been removed). However the condition \"retained children\" means children that have not been removed. So a node cannot be removed if it has any retained children.\n\nAdditionally: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Means after removing this node, the set of symbols in the remaining tree (including ancestors?) must still contain all required_symbols. So we need to check symbol occurrence. Since we are removing the node, we need to ensure that the required symbol is still present elsewhere. For root and other non-removable nodes, they are always retained. So as long as each required symbol appears in at least one retained node after removal, it's OK.\n\nThe process: We will maintain a set of retained node IDs initially all nodes. Then we iterate through removable nodes in order (depth descending, then id ascending). For each node, check if it is still present (retained), if it is removable (true), if it is not root, if its symbol is not required, if it has no retained children (i.e., all children's IDs are not in retained set), and after removal all required symbols remain represented (i.e., there is at least one retained node with that symbol for each required symbol). If all conditions met, we remove it: remove from retained set, add to removal order list. Continue until no more removals possible? Or we can do a single pass? Since removal of a node may enable removal of its parent later, we need to iterate until no more removals. The description \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" suggests we process in depth order, but if after removing some nodes, new candidates become eligible (parents become leaf), we should re-evaluate. So we need a loop: while removals happen, we re-scan? Or we can use a priority queue based on depth and id, and after removal, we can add parent to queue? Simpler: keep a sorted list of removable nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. Iterate through that list, but note that after removing a node, its parent might become eligible but it was already processed earlier (since parent has lower depth). So we need to re-scan after each removal? Or we can process depths in descending order and for each depth, repeatedly attempt removals until no more changes? Since removing a node doesn't affect nodes at same depth or higher depth (except its parent which is lower depth), we can process depths from max depth down to 1. For each depth, we can loop through nodes at that depth in id order, attempt removal, and if we remove a node, we should re-evaluate its parent (at lower depth) later. But the parent might have other children still present. So we need to re-check parent after each child removal. So perhaps better: maintain a list of removable nodes sorted by depth descending, id ascending. Then while list not empty, pop first node, check conditions, if removable then remove, else skip. But after removal, the parent's children count changes, and parent might become eligible later, but parent has lower depth so it will be later in the sorted list (if we process in descending depth, parent will appear after all deeper nodes). Since we pop from highest depth first, parent will be processed later anyway. However, if we pop a node, remove it, and later when we reach parent, we will check its conditions and may remove it. So a single pass through sorted list might be sufficient if we process nodes exactly once. But consider: a node has two children both removable. Remove first child, then later second child becomes removable (but it's already in the list after the first removal? The second child is at same depth, and we are processing in order; if we process children in id order, we will process the first child, remove it, then later the second child is still in the list and we will check it; its condition (no retained children) is still true because it has no children. So it's fine. However, what about a node that becomes removable only after all its children are removed? That node is at a lower depth, so after processing all its children (deeper nodes), we will eventually process it. So a single sorted scan works, provided we don't skip any nodes. But careful: the condition \"retained children\" means if node has any children still retained, cannot remove. Initially, a node with children can't be removed. After we remove all its children, it becomes eligible. But we are processing nodes in descending depth, so we will process all children before parent. So by the time we get to parent, its children have already been removed (or maybe not if some children are not removable). If some children are not removable, parent cannot be removed. So it's fine.\n\nThus algorithm: Input case is payload dict with keys: nodes (list of dicts), required_symbols (list), and _case_nonce (ignore). Build adjacency: parent -> children list. Also build dict node_id -> node info. Determine all node IDs. Initial retained set = all node ids. Removed list = [].\n\nGet max depth. Sort removable nodes (where removable is True) by depth descending, then id ascending. Iterate over that list. For each node id:\n- If node id not in retained set, skip (already removed)\n- Check if node depth == 0 or node's parent is None? Root is depth 0, parent null. But root is not removable anyway, but if removable flag is false, it won't be in list. So okay.\n- Check if node's symbol is in required_symbols: if yes, skip (cannot remove required symbol)\n- Check if any child of node is still in retained set: if yes, skip (has retained children)\n- Check if after removal, all required symbols are still present: For each required symbol, count nodes with that symbol in retained set (excluding this node). If any count becomes zero, skip. To optimize, we can precompute symbol counts, but simpler: for each required symbol, check if there exists at least one node in retained_set \\ {node} with that symbol.\n- If all conditions pass, remove node: remove from retained set, append node id to removed list.\n\nAfter loop, retained ids sorted (probably alphabetical order? Not specified but \"sorted retained IDs\" in description means return them sorted. Likely ascending string sort). Removed order is removal order (the order we appended).\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_list}.\n\nCheck example: In fixture payload a-000, nodes: root (depth0, not removable), n1 (depth1, symbol s1, not removable), n2 (depth1, s2, removable), n3 (depth1, s0, not removable), n4 (depth2, s1, removable, parent n2), n5 (depth2, s2, not removable), n6 (depth2, s0, removable, parent n1). Required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. Note: root is a symbol? root symbol is \"root\". So required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\".\n\nNow process removable nodes sorted by depth desc then id: depth2 nodes: n4 (id \"n4\"), n6 (id \"n6\"). n4: parent n2, symbol s1 (required!) -> cannot remove because required symbol. n6: parent n1, symbol s0 (not required), check children: none? n6 has no children? In nodes list, n6 is leaf, so no retained children. Check required symbols after removal: retained set includes root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Symbols: root from root, s1 from n1 and n4 (n4 still), s2 from n2 and n5, s0 from n3. So s1 still present (n1 and n4). So ok. Remove n6. Now depth1 nodes: n2 and n3? n1 is not removable. n2 (removable) at depth1: after removal of n6, parent n1 is still there but n2 itself has child n4 (still retained). Check retained children: n4 is still retained (since n4 not removed), so cannot remove n2. n3 is not removable. n4 not processed again because it's in list already and we skip it (already processed and not removed). So only n6 removed. Retained sorted: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root? Actually root is \"root\" id. Sorting strings: \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"root\" (since 'n' > 'r'? Actually \"root\" starts with 'r', \"n1\" starts with 'n'; 'n' < 'r', so sorted: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root? Wait \"root\" has 'r', 'n' < 'r', so \"n1\" etc come before \"root\". So order: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root). That seems plausible.\n\nNow we must ensure: After removal of n6, the node n1 still has child n6 removed, but n1 itself is not removable because removable: false. So tree shape changes but no further removals.\n\nThus implementation seems straightforward.\n\nEdge cases: If there are multiple required symbols, need to ensure each still exists.\n\nSorting retained IDs: should be sorted in ascending order (string comparison). Return list.\n\nNow we need to output implementation as a Python source code string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The Python source must define `solve(case)`. Must not mutate case. Must use only standard library. Must return JSON-serializable.\n\nWe'll write code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The input case may have nodes list with different fields; we must handle. The depth field is provided; we could compute depth from parent if needed but we have depth. Use given depth.\n\nThe condition \"removable\" is boolean field. Only consider nodes where removable == True.\n\n\"Never remove the root\" - root is depth 0 and parent null, but ensure we don't remove even if some error allows removable true? But fixture shows root removable false.\n\n\"Never remove a required symbol\" - we check if symbol in required_symbols.\n\n\"Never remove a node with retained children\" - check if any child has id in retained set.\n\nWe'll pre-build a children adjacency dict: for each node, children list from parent references.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    # build maps\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # initial retained set\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # sort removable nodes\n    removable_nodes = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"removable\"]]\n    # sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    removable_nodes.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid][\"depth\"], nid))\n    # process\n    for nid in removable_nodes:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        # check conditions\n        # never remove root (depth 0 or parent null)\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        # never remove required symbol\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required:\n            continue\n        # never remove node with retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols remain after removal\n        # compute if any retained node (excluding this) has each required symbol\n        # we can precompute symbol count or just check\n        # Since retained set is small, we can iterate over required and check existence\n        # Avoid using set difference for large? fine.\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required:\n            # check if any node (other than current) in retained has symbol\n            found = False\n            for rid in retained:\n                if rid == nid:\n                    continue\n                if node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] == sym:\n                    found = True\n                    break\n            if not found:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # all conditions passed, remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    # sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nThat should work.\n\nBut note: The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" also applies if the node has children that are not in retained set? That's fine. Also, after we remove a node, we don't need to update children list.\n\nTest with example a-000: We'll trust.\n\nWe also need to ensure we don't mutate `case`. The code doesn't modify case.\n\nNow produce JSON output: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped Python code>\"}. Escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nWrite the Python code as a string. Use triple quotes? No, we need to embed it in JSON string. So we will write the Python code with proper indentation and then escape.\n\nWe'll generate the response.\n\nBut also consider: The problem says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`.\" So our entire response must be that JSON.\n\nWe'll produce that.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":3797,"total_tokens":5851,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3413},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}